WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.060 --> 00:00:07.660
Computer-generated imagery or CGI has allowed for a massive range of new stories to be told through movies and TV.

00:00:07.660 --> 00:00:10.620
Black holes, dragons, superheroes.

00:00:10.620 --> 00:00:11.440
"Oh my!"

00:00:11.440 --> 00:00:14.600
I used to think CGI was some sort of mysterious nerd magic

00:00:14.600 --> 00:00:17.900
that can only be made by a powerful supercomputer at a Hollywood studio.

00:00:17.940 --> 00:00:21.840
However, I began my own visual effects career when I realized this wasn't the case.

00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:26.760
I realized that this was an actual career path for thousands of artists across the world.

00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:32.900
I now work for a small production studio called Corridor Digital and we're known for using visual effects to make fun short films on YouTube.

00:00:37.800 --> 00:00:39.880
"You have to go faster!"

00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:42.924
I love bringing things to life that didn't previously exist.

00:00:42.924 --> 00:00:44.920
At the heart of that for me are visual effects.

00:00:44.920 --> 00:00:46.580
I am a big fan.

00:00:46.580 --> 00:00:50.100
However, not everyone shares my excitement about that and I get it. It's fine.

00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:52.160
Sometimes the effects just suck.

00:00:52.720 --> 00:00:56.440
Sometimes you can just tell that it's fake and that's a bit of a bummer.

00:00:56.440 --> 00:00:57.680
You watch movies to enjoy stories,

00:00:57.699 --> 00:01:00.340
but when the CGI is bad, it can take you out of it.

00:01:00.340 --> 00:01:01.479
Last year, for instance,

00:01:01.480 --> 00:01:04.680
I walked out of the theater having just seen Black Panther

00:01:04.680 --> 00:01:06.220
Now, I really enjoyed this movie.

00:01:06.229 --> 00:01:09.189
However, I was disappointed by the quality of CGI,

00:01:09.740 --> 00:01:13.089
particularly in the final fight scene and it turned out I wasn't alone either.

00:01:13.330 --> 00:01:15.900
I asked Twitter what movies they thought had bad effects,

00:01:15.900 --> 00:01:18.399
and they overwhelmingly responded with Black Panther.

00:01:18.399 --> 00:01:21.680
I am hesitant to just openly criticize the effects of the movie

00:01:21.680 --> 00:01:23.919
because a lot of overworked artists helped create them.

00:01:23.990 --> 00:01:25.880
And I think about those artists a lot.

00:01:25.880 --> 00:01:27.620
But you know who doesn't think about them?

00:01:27.620 --> 00:01:28.761
Basically everyone else.

00:01:28.761 --> 00:01:30.680
You see there's a common misconception

00:01:30.680 --> 00:01:32.800
regarding how CGI is created.

00:01:32.800 --> 00:01:36.374
People tend to think effects are just made by computers rather than actual people,

00:01:36.374 --> 00:01:37.420
like an Instagram filter.

00:01:37.600 --> 00:01:39.600
They think we just press a button and poof!

00:01:40.310 --> 00:01:42.310
Movie magic.

00:01:43.039 --> 00:01:47.640
Hollywood VFX pipeline, however, is really complicated

00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:50.049
First, you need proper onset data acquisition to feed to the camera tracking department

00:01:50.050 --> 00:01:53.199
where a bunch of sculptors will then build detailed 3d models to send off to

00:01:53.270 --> 00:01:55.580
Texture artists to make them look real.

00:01:55.580 --> 00:02:00.369
And once lighting is set, render farms are managed 24 hours a day to spit out clean looking CGI,

00:02:00.369 --> 00:02:06.280
only to lay it on a compositors desk who performs video Photoshop to blend everything into a final image.

00:02:07.380 --> 00:02:09.240
And if you didn't get all that, it's alright.

00:02:09.240 --> 00:02:13.680
Just know that every one of these steps can only begin once the previous step has been completed.

00:02:13.690 --> 00:02:16.740
But if any of those steps break down,

00:02:16.740 --> 00:02:18.729
those next in line are stuck polishing a turd, as we say in the industry.

00:02:18.920 --> 00:02:21.640
You'd be surprised how often that happens.

00:02:21.640 --> 00:02:24.940
And yet, visual effects are so ubiquitous now that they just don't wow us anymore.

00:02:24.940 --> 00:02:26.445
We've become desensitized.

00:02:26.445 --> 00:02:29.109
And while not everyone is an artist, everyone is a critic.

00:02:29.740 --> 00:02:32.960
If anything is slightly off, audiences can tell it looks fake,

00:02:32.960 --> 00:02:34.294
even if they don't know how.

00:02:34.294 --> 00:02:35.199
So what tips us off?

00:02:36.530 --> 00:02:39.760
Well, the first sign is that it simply isn't photo real.

00:02:39.760 --> 00:02:41.559
The lighting or materials don't represent reality well enough

00:02:41.560 --> 00:02:43.220
to look like a real photograph.

00:02:43.220 --> 00:02:46.320
It's a simple idea but a pretty tall order

00:02:46.320 --> 00:02:49.690
and it's a little unfair even, because we all have a lot of experience with reality.

00:02:50.320 --> 00:02:52.310
We see it every waking moment

00:02:52.310 --> 00:02:56.740
Everything that you can see has a material property that defines how it reacts to light

00:02:56.740 --> 00:02:58.327
and therefore how you observe it

00:02:58.327 --> 00:02:59.170
Things like color,

00:02:59.390 --> 00:03:00.280
reflectivity,

00:03:00.280 --> 00:03:01.390
physical texture,

00:03:01.550 --> 00:03:02.280
transparency.

00:03:02.280 --> 00:03:06.580
Skin for example is slightly transparent and absorbs the light that hits it.

00:03:06.580 --> 00:03:08.480
You don't realize it but you know

00:03:08.480 --> 00:03:11.200
exactly what all these visible properties are

00:03:11.200 --> 00:03:12.200
without even having to think about it.

00:03:12.310 --> 00:03:15.040
So when they're off, you can tell.

00:03:15.040 --> 00:03:18.040
And it's not just about how CGI looks but how it moves, too.

00:03:18.040 --> 00:03:21.380
Bad CGI can oftentimes look photo-real when paused,

00:03:21.380 --> 00:03:23.120
but in motion looks wrong.

00:03:23.120 --> 00:03:26.080
There are three main ways we replicate motion in the digital world

00:03:26.080 --> 00:03:27.560
and one is animation,

00:03:27.560 --> 00:03:28.899
where the motion is designed from scratch.

00:03:29.840 --> 00:03:31.540
Another is through simulation

00:03:31.540 --> 00:03:35.709
which is putting some rules into a program and then letting it decide where things should fall.

00:03:35.870 --> 00:03:37.820
And finally, there is motion capture

00:03:37.820 --> 00:03:40.920
which records the movements of an actor in order to most accurately replicate

00:03:40.920 --> 00:03:42.100
the subtle ways in which they move.

00:03:42.470 --> 00:03:44.120
Depending on the film,

00:03:44.120 --> 00:03:46.000
usually a combination of these three techniques are used

00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:48.880
And the recent Planet of the Apes movies are actually great examples of this.

00:03:49.160 --> 00:03:52.389
Motion capture provides the core movement of the skeleton and face,

00:03:52.519 --> 00:03:55.420
something that's super challenging to create without reference.

00:03:55.420 --> 00:03:58.320
Those gray skin suits aren't just fashion statements after all.

00:03:58.320 --> 00:03:59.439
But add it on top of that,

00:03:59.440 --> 00:04:04.180
are simulations of muscle and hair that actually react to the motion of the performer.

00:04:04.180 --> 00:04:04.690
When added together,

00:04:04.690 --> 00:04:06.300
this looks more natural,

00:04:06.300 --> 00:04:08.049
but that's not to say that there isn't any human intervention.

00:04:08.720 --> 00:04:12.220
Animators usually have to step in to make alterations so that not only everything works together,

00:04:12.220 --> 00:04:14.520
but that it fits the vision of the director.

00:04:14.520 --> 00:04:16.724
And the results are exciting because through this,

00:04:16.724 --> 00:04:17.649
we can create digital

00:04:17.780 --> 00:04:20.200
characters that perform like real people.

00:04:20.200 --> 00:04:22.390
Now these days CGI is usually pretty solid.

00:04:22.390 --> 00:04:25.569
We've gotten good enough at rendering and moving things to pass initial inspection.

00:04:25.660 --> 00:04:30.220
But there is one area of scrutiny where only absolute perfection is acceptable.

00:04:31.040 --> 00:04:33.160
Realistic human faces.

00:04:33.160 --> 00:04:35.920
People can scrutinize faces to an incredible degree.

00:04:36.100 --> 00:04:39.020
I mean, they are the defining feature in how we tell each other apart.

00:04:39.320 --> 00:04:41.460
So when we try to create human faces,

00:04:41.460 --> 00:04:43.409
we can experience what is called the "uncanny valley".

00:04:45.099 --> 00:04:49.620
This graph plots our emotional response against the realism of a fake person.

00:04:49.620 --> 00:04:52.769
The far-left signifies characters that are super obviously not real people

00:04:52.770 --> 00:04:55.260
so there's an emotional disconnect.

00:04:55.260 --> 00:04:57.479
But as they get more realistic, we begin to connect more.

00:04:57.849 --> 00:05:00.480
Gollum from Lord of the Rings, for instance,

00:05:00.480 --> 00:05:02.339
is very lifelike and we can even connect with him as a person.

00:05:02.800 --> 00:05:07.160
But his proportions are all wrong, so you're never fooled into thinking he's actually human.

00:05:07.160 --> 00:05:09.030
The familiarity just isn't fully there.

00:05:09.129 --> 00:05:12.700
It's the same reason why we enjoy animated movies like The Incredibles,

00:05:12.700 --> 00:05:14.549
but feel disturbed when watching The Polar Express.

00:05:15.190 --> 00:05:19.080
You see something weird happens when characters begin to get this realistic.

00:05:19.080 --> 00:05:21.809
They fall into the uncanny valley and we can have an adverse reaction.

00:05:22.260 --> 00:05:24.480
I'm willing to bet you won't describe this as cute.

00:05:24.820 --> 00:05:25.620
"Meow"

00:05:25.700 --> 00:05:29.400
And there have been many examples of human CGI characters that fall into this valley.

00:05:29.400 --> 00:05:32.219
In fact, it's something that artists are still struggling with today.

00:05:32.770 --> 00:05:34.780
Even Star Wars Rogue One,

00:05:34.780 --> 00:05:39.780
which has some of the most realistic digital humans ever created failed to be absolutely perfect.

00:05:40.330 --> 00:05:44.819
We may not be disturbed anymore, but we can still instinctually identify something is off

00:05:45.120 --> 00:05:48.179
I mean maybe its the lighting or the eyes or the motion of the lips.

00:05:48.610 --> 00:05:49.110
Honestly,

00:05:49.110 --> 00:05:51.479
I don't know.

00:05:51.479 --> 00:05:55.288
Currently, I can only think of two examples that truly succeeded in climbing out of the uncanny valley.

00:05:55.479 --> 00:05:57.479
Take a look at this shot from the movie Logan.

00:06:05.159 --> 00:06:08.640
You may have figured this younger Wolverine was the result of some,

00:06:08.640 --> 00:06:09.558
you know, simple digital makeup.

00:06:09.560 --> 00:06:12.780
But nope, Hugh Jackman's face was 100%

00:06:12.780 --> 00:06:14.900
recreated and rendered.

00:06:14.900 --> 00:06:18.220
And this just blows my mind cuz there are dozens more examples like this in the movie

00:06:18.229 --> 00:06:19.739
but no one could tell.

00:06:19.739 --> 00:06:23.180
The other example is Rachel from Blade Runner 2049

00:06:23.180 --> 00:06:25.140
and this is a character from the original movie,

00:06:25.140 --> 00:06:26.388
but is CGI in the sequel.

00:06:27.929 --> 00:06:32.540
The quest to make her unquestionably real took the effects team over a year

00:06:32.540 --> 00:06:33.798
and the results were so convincing

00:06:33.799 --> 00:06:35.929
I had no idea she wasn't real

00:06:36.509 --> 00:06:41.328
Blade Runner 2049 ended up winning the Academy Award for Best Visual Effects in 2018

00:06:41.329 --> 00:06:43.319
and at first I was honestly a little disappointed.

00:06:43.320 --> 00:06:46.020
You see, I thought War for the Planet of the Apes

00:06:46.020 --> 00:06:50.860
deserved the Oscar because creating a cast of lifelike characters is an incredible technical achievement.

00:06:50.869 --> 00:06:52.680
But here's the thing:

00:06:52.680 --> 00:06:55.789
great visual effects are about more than just technical achievement.

00:06:55.789 --> 00:06:59.140
It has to be about artistic expression as well.

00:06:59.140 --> 00:07:01.522
And that is something 2049 had in spades.

00:07:01.522 --> 00:07:02.509
I mean that movie

00:07:02.509 --> 00:07:04.509
Oh that movie had an aesthetic!

00:07:04.799 --> 00:07:08.059
And Academy voters were swayed by the visual artistry.

00:07:08.159 --> 00:07:11.520
That, and some voters apparently really thought that

00:07:11.520 --> 00:07:12.020
the Planet of the Apes crew were just really good at getting the Apes to behave

00:07:12.020 --> 00:07:14.840
so well on camera.

00:07:15.260 --> 00:07:16.183
Remember,

00:07:16.183 --> 00:07:18.440
we just press buttons.

00:07:18.449 --> 00:07:23.449
But for real though movies like these push the bounds of Technology and managed to do one very important thing

00:07:23.969 --> 00:07:25.969
Create the ability to tell better stories

00:07:26.610 --> 00:07:30.379
At the end of the day that is the most important role of great visual effects

00:07:30.379 --> 00:07:35.539
They need to serve the story when the effects of the movie are flashy just for the sake of being flashy

00:07:35.539 --> 00:07:38.868
It doesn't serve any purpose other than to create a spectacle event

00:07:38.869 --> 00:07:44.659
You know what? I'm talking about the disaster movies the monster flicks the Transformers films the Justice League

00:07:46.799 --> 00:07:50.119
More and more movies are relying on CGI to hide a weak plot

00:07:50.119 --> 00:07:55.369
And that's causing audiences to turn their noses up at they desire movies with real practical effects

00:07:55.740 --> 00:08:00.229
And I understand knowing that what you're seeing is real and tangible can be exciting

00:08:00.300 --> 00:08:03.680
But I think what people are really craving is merely suspension of disbelief

00:08:04.350 --> 00:08:07.070
When they notice CGI the illusion is shattered

00:08:07.070 --> 00:08:09.070
So they naturally just want less of that

00:08:09.240 --> 00:08:14.269
Director Christopher Nolan agrees with the sentiment and strives to capture as much as possible in camera

00:08:14.420 --> 00:08:19.099
He claims he only uses visual effects as a last resort, but I believe that's not really the case

00:08:19.160 --> 00:08:26.330
oh, I have no doubt and if he could have dropped Anne Hathaway into a wormhole or burn Aaron Eckhart's face off he would have

00:08:27.180 --> 00:08:28.040
No, no

00:08:28.040 --> 00:08:33.799
he just simply knows that CGI is a tool and knows exactly how to use that tool to make you forget that you're watching a

00:08:33.800 --> 00:08:39.320
Movie that was the magic of Jurassic Park this movie came out 26 years ago and the effects are so good

00:08:39.320 --> 00:08:42.530
They hold them today, but do they though do they really?

00:08:48.000 --> 00:08:50.030
Notice anything weird about this shot

00:08:53.370 --> 00:08:56.179
All right, yes the rapture disappears for one frame

00:08:56.180 --> 00:09:00.710
And yes, that's actually in the movie. The effects were groundbreaking at the time

00:09:00.710 --> 00:09:05.359
But if you really go back and look at only the CGI, I think you'll find that they really don't hold up

00:09:05.360 --> 00:09:11.120
It just doesn't matter because they were used in such creative ways that for the first time in history a movie made you believe

00:09:11.460 --> 00:09:13.460
That dinosaurs were real

00:09:15.140 --> 00:09:21.259
And I think that that's something that the recent sequels failed to do because on a technical level Jurassic world has far superior CGI

00:09:21.260 --> 00:09:26.720
But it's never really grounded in reality. It just feels like it was too preoccupied with whether it could use CGI

00:09:26.720 --> 00:09:28.620
It didn't stop to think of it should

00:09:28.620 --> 00:09:35.630
Normally though you only ever notice CGI when it is been in fact, the best visual effects are never noticed to begin with they're invisible

00:09:36.990 --> 00:09:40.790
David Fincher is such a perfectionist when it comes to creative control that he would rather

00:09:40.860 --> 00:09:44.960
Create digital blood than to leave it up to chance by splashing it around in real life

00:09:44.960 --> 00:09:47.119
The steam is just so good at it. You never noticed

00:09:47.580 --> 00:09:51.139
Did you know that is movie this social network has a thousand VFX shots in it?

00:09:51.140 --> 00:09:51.620
Yeah

00:09:51.620 --> 00:09:56.480
The Facebook movie has more V effects than the 2014 Godzilla movie like what?

00:09:56.970 --> 00:10:00.439
turning one person into twins who interact with each other is pretty technical stuff that

00:10:00.540 --> 00:10:03.529
hundreds of artists worked very hard to make sure you wouldn't notice but

00:10:03.750 --> 00:10:08.420
There is one final factor that can really dictate the quality of CGI and that is time

00:10:09.090 --> 00:10:15.439
The biggest issue with effects today isn't the talent of the artist creating them but the time needed to do so

00:10:15.720 --> 00:10:22.819
However, moviemaking is a business and more time means more money and release dates are incredibly important to the bottom line of a blockbuster

00:10:23.420 --> 00:10:29.119
So films trying to meet deadlines can become rushed and this was ultimately the problem with Black Panther

00:10:29.730 --> 00:10:34.670
now it's important to note that most of the effects in this movie are actually great, but there were

00:10:35.610 --> 00:10:42.950
2,500 of them and even though the work was divided between 13 different VFX studios there simply wasn't enough time to do everything properly

00:10:43.430 --> 00:10:46.670
They were refilling the final fight scene in October

00:10:46.680 --> 00:10:51.530
But the movie still had to come out in February which left only a matter of weeks

00:10:51.660 --> 00:10:55.519
To do all of the effects for that entire scene from scratch

00:10:56.160 --> 00:11:01.670
The company that took on this challenge has even won the VFX Oscars for all four of these movies

00:11:02.079 --> 00:11:04.079
the results prove that great CGI

00:11:04.100 --> 00:11:09.999
Cannot be rushed like all forms of art quality comes down to a function of skill and time the greatest

00:11:10.069 --> 00:11:14.409
Renaissance artist took years to complete their paintings and if I try to paint something great

00:11:14.410 --> 00:11:19.540
It wouldn't matter how much time I spent because I lack the skill and I think you'll find that even great

00:11:19.670 --> 00:11:22.329
Artists would deliver shoddy work when rushed to complete it

00:11:22.730 --> 00:11:29.949
So in the movie industry where time is finite artists have to strike a balance no movie has ever finished. It just gets released

00:11:30.679 --> 00:11:32.679
So the next time you're watching a summer blockbuster?

00:11:32.989 --> 00:11:37.899
Even if the visuals fall flat take a moment to appreciate all of the hard work that went unnoticed

00:11:38.509 --> 00:11:45.399
visual effects artists are artists after all they pour their blood sweat and tears into these stories that capture our

00:11:45.709 --> 00:11:50.738
Imaginations and sometimes even their best work may have been invisible all along

